### Financial constraints

Multiple empirical studies identify upfront capital requirements and uncertainty about returns as central deterrents to household rooftop solar adoption. The Kerala study cited by Bagla reports that respondents most frequently raised financial concerns when discussing barriers, indicating that affordability often dominates environmental motivation in investment decisions. {{cite:S1#c1}} The same review notes long payback expectations and limited access to tailored financing as recurring themes in the literature. {{cite:S1#c1}}

Lack of awareness about available financing mechanisms magnifies perceived unaffordability. National survey evidence discussed in the literature finds that among households who considered rooftop solar “expensive” yet remained uninterested, a large majority reported no knowledge of financing options, implying that informational gaps about finance can be as important as headline subsidy levels for uptake. {{cite:S1#c1}} Qualitative work in Karnataka likewise frames adoption failure as a conversion problem: many households express positive attitudes toward solar but stop short of installation because accumulated frictions—including those affecting subsidy access and payment arrangements—raise the effective financial burden. {{cite:S1#c6}} 

Policy design shapes the economic case for rooftop systems in concrete ways. National schemes that offer direct subsidies can materially reduce initial capital needs—for example, PM Surya Ghar provides subsidies of up to INR 78,000 for systems at or above 3 kW—but other regulatory requirements and equipment-cost drivers may offset these gains for price-sensitive households. {{cite:S1#c1}} Cross-state analysis reinforces this complexity: comparisons of installation levels and subsidy generosity show weak or even negative associations between subsidy magnitude and uptake, suggesting that how incentives are delivered and experienced matters more than their nominal size. {{cite:S2#c8}}

The subsidy itself can create procedural friction that undermines its intended effect. Field evidence points to a “subsidy paradox” in which vendor-mediated, complex application procedures increase delays and opacity, thereby elevating perceived transaction costs and reducing uptake. {{cite:S1#c3}} In Karnataka, respondents link procedural slowdowns (for example, in approvals and documentation) to hesitation and drop-out, which converts what should be financial support into an additional barrier. {{cite:S1#c7}}

Several studies recommend addressing financial constraints by improving household financial literacy and simplifying the subsidy-to-installation pathway. Proposals include standardised finance information packages (EMI schedules, payback and bill-reduction calculators) and closer partnerships with banks and microfinance institutions to expand credit options for low-consumption households; such measures aim to reduce the salience of high upfront cost and clarify the investment proposition. {{cite:S1#c2}} [[NEEDS SOURCE: empirical estimates of credit uptake after interventions]]

