Household decisions about rooftop solar in Karnataka are strongly shaped by upfront affordability concerns and limited access to suitable finance. Multiple studies identify high initial capital requirements, long perceived payback periods, and uncertainty about returns as central deterrents to adoption. {{cite:S1#c1}} Evidence from a national survey indicates that what households call “expensive” systems often reflects lack of knowledge about how to finance them: among households that perceived rooftop solar as costly but were aware of the technology, 73 per cent reported not knowing available financing options. {{cite:S1#c1}} These gaps in financing awareness mean that subsidy levels alone may not convert intent into installation when potential adopters cannot translate subsidies into clear, accessible payment paths. {{cite:S1#c1}}

Subsidy design both alleviates and introduces financial friction. Central support such as PM Surya Ghar reduces gross capital requirements by offering up to INR 78,000 for systems of 3 kW and above, which can materially lower the barrier for some buyers. {{cite:S1#c1}} However, the same literature documents a “subsidy paradox” where the mechanisms intended to lower cost instead raise procedural complexity and delay, increasing perceived risk and administrative burden for households. {{cite:S1#c3}} The qualitative work in Karnataka finds that this institutional interface—the set of steps through which a household must navigate to claim financial support—frequently generates the frictions that stop installations even among motivated households. {{cite:S1#c6}} Respondents specifically reported delays and documentation hurdles from the local distribution company, reinforcing that procedural slippage, not only headline subsidy amounts, shapes affordability in practice. {{cite:S1#c7}}

Cross-state evidence reinforces that nominal financial incentives are insufficient by themselves. Inter-state comparisons show substantial variation in rooftop adoption that does not track subsidy generosity; regression and ANOVA results reported weak relationships between installations and standard economic variables, and in one analysis higher subsidy percentages correlated negatively with adoption levels. {{cite:S2#c8}} This pattern supports the interpretation that the form and delivery of finance matter at least as much as its magnitude. {{cite:S2#c8}}

Policy-oriented evaluations therefore emphasise interventions aimed at lowering perceived financial risk and improving financing uptake rather than only increasing subsidy size. Suggested measures include standardized consumer-facing finance tools (EMI options, payback calculators, bill-saving estimates), stronger linkages with banks and microfinance, and models tailored to small consumers such as shared or subscription-based offerings. {{cite:S1#c2}} In Karnataka, the accumulated evidence implies that improving the transparency and user‑friendliness of subsidy pathways and expanding accessible finance products are central to reducing the financial constraints that currently suppress rooftop solar uptake. {{cite:S1#c3}}{{cite:S1#c6}}

