### 

Empirical evidence in the literature frames subsidies as necessary but insufficient for household rooftop solar adoption. The review finds that policy support creates conditions that enable uptake but does not by itself convert willingness into completed installations. {{cite:S1#c4}}

Several studies identify high upfront cost and limited access to finance as central financial barriers to adoption, with affordability concerns frequently outweighing environmental motivations. {{cite:S1#c1}} The same work reports that many households who judge rooftop solar to be expensive are actually unaware of financing alternatives, indicating that gaps in financial literacy and knowledge of credit options contribute to low uptake. {{cite:S1#c1}} Where subsidies reduce the headline capital requirement, other policy features and market regulations can offset those gains for price-sensitive consumers. {{cite:S1#c1}}

State- and national-level analyses find that larger nominal subsidies do not reliably translate into higher adoption rates. A descriptive and regression analysis across states showed weak links between economic variables and installations and documented a counterintuitive negative correlation between subsidy percentage and observed uptake. {{cite:S2#c8}} These results imply that the design, delivery, and perceived reliability of incentives often matter more than subsidy size alone. {{cite:S2#c8}}

Qualitative evidence from Karnataka reinforces this conclusion by showing how procedural and institutional frictions around subsidy access impede conversion from interest to installation. Households report delays with distribution companies, complex documentation requirements, and uncertainty over net-metering approval times as recurrent obstacles in the subsidy pathway. {{cite:S1#c7}} The Karnataka study interprets these cumulative frictions as the critical barrier: households do not reject the technology as much as they encounter an onerous institutional interface when attempting to access support. {{cite:S1#c6}} 

The literature also specifies a subsidy paradox: the process of obtaining subsidised support sometimes creates additional friction that discourages adoption. {{cite:S1#c3}} Suggested remedies aim at simplifying the subsidy interaction—for example, permitting end-users to apply directly, introducing stepwise checklist forms, or revising residential procurement requirements to reduce cost pressure—measures intended to reduce administrative opacity and delays. {{cite:S1#c3}} 

Policy commentary emphasises that tackling perception and informational frictions may multiply the impact of existing subsidies. Targeted communication to lower anticipated procedural complexity and standardised financial information tools are proposed to improve decision-making and financing uptake among households. {{cite:S1#c2}} Together, these lines of evidence portray subsidy experience not as a single monetary parameter but as a multifaceted interface: monetary support matters, yet its practical effect depends on transparency, procedural simplicity, and household awareness. {{cite:S1#c4}}{{cite:S2#c8}}

