### Subsidy experience

Subsidies and supportive regulations create enabling conditions for rooftop solar, but they do not by themselves secure household installations; the literature frames adoption as constrained by multiple interacting frictions rather than technology alone. {{cite:S1#c4}}

Qualitative evidence from Karnataka illustrates how these frictions play out at the household level: a study that interviewed 70 households reports that adoption stalls because obstacles accumulate across the adoption pathway—awareness, consideration, initiation, installation and post-installation—so that interest often fails to convert into completed systems. {{cite:S1#c6}} 

A distinctive theme in the Karnataka evidence is a “subsidy paradox,” where the subsidy mechanism increases procedural friction instead of reducing it. Respondents describe delays, complex documentation and opaque institutional steps—particularly around discom interactions and net-metering approvals—that raise the effective cost (in time, uncertainty and trust) of claiming support. {{cite:S1#c3}}{{cite:S1#c7}}

Financial support on paper can therefore be insufficient when households lack clear, trusted pathways to access it. Survey evidence at the national level finds that affordability concerns frequently reflect limited awareness of financing options rather than price alone: among households who perceived rooftop solar as expensive but were not interested, 73 per cent were unaware of financing products. {{cite:S1#c1}} The same review also notes that programme design affects financial attractiveness; for example, PM Surya Ghar offers up to INR 78,000 for systems of 3 kW and above, which materially reduces upfront requirements for qualifying systems. {{cite:S1#c1}}

Cross-state descriptive and regression analyses further complicate a simple subsidy‑value story: some lower-subsidy states outperform higher-subsidy peers, and a reported negative correlation between subsidy percentage and installation counts suggests that nominal subsidy size alone does not predict uptake. The work concludes that delivery quality, administrative ease and perceived reliability of incentives can matter more than headline subsidy levels. {{cite:S2#c8}}

Policy-oriented recommendations emerging from the evidence therefore emphasise reducing procedural friction and improving end-user-facing communication and finance literacy: proposals include enabling direct end-user subsidy applications, simplifying checklists and forms, deploying demonstration sites and testimonials, and providing standardized “Solar Finance Kits” with EMI options and payback calculators to raise clarity and trust. {{cite:S1#c3}}{{cite:S1#c2}}

