Karnataka households’ experience with subsidies shows that financial incentives alone do not ensure rooftop solar uptake. The qualitative study finds that adoption stalls because friction accumulates across the adoption journey, even where policy frameworks are progressive, producing a large conversion gap between awareness and installation. {{cite:S1#c6}} The study links these household-level frictions to the “last mile” of subsidy delivery: barriers in the institutional interface, not subsidy availability, often prevent households from completing installations. {{cite:S1#c7}}

Several pieces of evidence explain why subsidies fail to translate into uptake. First, affordability concerns frequently reflect lack of knowledge about financing rather than absolute cost, as a national survey found that 73 per cent of households who perceived rooftop solar as expensive were unaware of financing options. {{cite:S1#c1}} Second, policy design and parallel regulations can blunt subsidy benefits: while a central scheme can offer up to INR 78,000 for systems of 3 kW and above, rising equipment costs driven by domestic content and ALMM rules may offset that support for price-sensitive buyers. {{cite:S1#c1}} Third, procedural complexity around application, vendor selection, and net-metering approvals generates delays and discourages action even among motivated households. {{cite:S1#c7}}

Comparative and quantitative evidence reinforces the limited causal power of subsidy size. An inter-state analysis reports wide variation in rooftop installations that does not align with subsidy generosity; in fact, it identifies a negative correlation between subsidy percentage and adoption levels and no significant difference in installations between high- and low-subsidy states. {{cite:S2#c8}} This pattern implies that the mechanism and perceived reliability of incentives, plus non-financial frictions, can outweigh nominal subsidy amounts. {{cite:S2#c8}}

The literature and the Karnataka fieldwork converge on both diagnosis and policy directions: simplifying subsidy access and improving financial literacy could reduce the friction that subsidies inadvertently introduce. The studies document a “subsidy paradox,” where vendor-mediated and complex application processes increase opacity and delay, and they recommend testing direct end-user applications, checklist-based forms, and revisions to ALMM requirements to lower cost anxieties without compromising quality. {{cite:S1#c3}}{{cite:S1#c2}} Together, these findings narrow the subsidy question to delivery: effective subsidies must be paired with clear, low-friction pathways and finance-awareness measures to convert interest into installations. {{cite:S1#c4}}

