Subsidies create enabling conditions for rooftop solar but do not by themselves ensure household uptake, because the conversion from willingness to installation depends on how households experience the subsidy delivery process. {{cite:S1#c4}}

Household-level evidence from Karnataka shows that financial concerns remain central: high upfront costs, uncertain returns, and limited financing access consistently deter adoption. {{cite:S1#c1}} The CEEW finding that 73 per cent of households who perceived rooftop solar as expensive were unaware of financing options illustrates that affordability often reflects financing awareness rather than only sticker price. {{cite:S1#c1}} National policy instruments such as PM Surya Ghar reduce initial capital requirements through grants up to INR 78,000 for systems of 3 kW and above, but equipment-cost drivers linked to domestic content and ALMM rules may erode some of those gains for price-sensitive buyers. {{cite:S1#c1}}

Qualitative evidence from Karnataka indicates that subsidy-related procedures can introduce additional friction: bureaucratic complexity, vendor-mediated applications, and uncertain approval timelines slow or deter households even when subsidies are available. {{cite:S1#c6}}{{cite:S1#c7}} Empirical recommendations propose simplifying subsidy access by allowing end-users to apply directly, introducing checklist-based forms, and reconsidering ALMM requirements for residential systems to lower cost anxiety without compromising quality. {{cite:S1#c3}}

Perceptual and informational frictions interact with subsidy design: anticipatory anxiety about procedural complexity often exceeds actual complexity, suggesting that targeted communication and demonstration of adopter experiences could reduce hesitation and improve the practical uptake of subsidised schemes. {{cite:S1#c2}} Policy experiments recommended in the literature include a “Solar Finance Kit” to standardise EMI options and payback calculators and hyperlocal outreach via panchayats and DISCOM field staff to build trust and awareness that complement subsidy offers. {{cite:S1#c2}}

Comparative evidence across states further questions the assumption that larger nominal subsidies automatically raise installations: inter-state analysis finds weak links between subsidy percentages and uptake, with some low-subsidy states outperforming higher-subsidy counterparts, and a negative correlation between subsidy percentage and installations in one descriptive study. {{cite:S2#c8}} This pattern implies that the form, delivery, and perceived reliability of subsidies are critical to household response, not just their headline value. {{cite:S2#c8}}

