Quantitative analyses across states highlight this disconnect between nominal subsidy generosity and actual uptake. One multi‑state study reports substantial inter‑state variation in rooftop installations that does not track subsidy size, and finds a weak relationship between conventional economic predictors and adoption; notably the analysis documents a negative correlation between subsidy percentage and uptake, implying that larger subsidies alone do not guarantee higher installations. {{cite:S2#c8}} This result supports the view that the form, reliability and administrative quality of incentives matter at least as much as their headline value.

Qualitative evidence from Karnataka elaborates the mechanisms through which subsidies fail to translate into adoption. Households in the Karnataka study commonly encounter delays and procedural complexity—application processing by DISCOMs, documentation burdens, and uncertainty over net‑metering approvals—which discourage completion even among motivated adopters. {{cite:S1#c7}} The study’s sample composition and design focused on these “last‑mile” interactions, comparing experiences of adopters, serious considerers and non‑adopters to trace where friction accumulates. {{cite:S1#c5}}{{cite:S1#c6}}

Financially, the literature shows two linked issues: affordability and awareness of financing instruments. Large upfront costs remain salient, but part of the affordability problem reflects low awareness of credit and payment options; for instance, a survey found that among households who perceived rooftop systems as expensive but were uninterested, 73 per cent did not know about available financing alternatives. {{cite:S1#c1}} Policy features do alter the cash barrier—programs such as PM Surya Ghar provide explicit per‑system subsidy caps (up to INR 78,000 for systems ≥3 kW)—yet other regulatory costs or equipment‑specification rules can blunt those gains for price‑sensitive consumers. {{cite:S1#c1}}

Several applied recommendations emerge directly from this evidence: reducing the subsidy paradox by enabling household‑led subsidy applications, simplifying forms into checklist steps, and reconsidering residential ALMM requirements; and addressing finance‑related informational gaps through standardized “Solar Finance Kits,” stronger bank partnerships, and targeted communication to reduce anticipatory complexity. {{cite:S1#c3}}{{cite:S1#c2}} These measures target the institutional and informational frictions that the empirical work identifies as central obstacles to converting subsidy availability into rooftop installations. {{cite:S1#c4}}

